---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SignatureDiagram from '../components/SignatureDiagram.astro';
import {
  checks,
  dependencies,
  docs,
  reporting,
  SDK_REPO,
  specifications,
} from '../lib/assurance.mjs';
import { maturityLine } from '../lib/sdk.mjs';

const DOCS = 'https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main';

/*
 * Every row here is checked against docs/SECURITY.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, and the
 * Envelope type in remote/relay/types.ts. If the SDK changes what it puts on
 * the wire, this table is wrong and must be corrected — it is the page the
 * whole brand is staked on.
 */
const envelopeFields = [
  { field: 'targetUserId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'Who the message is for' },
  { field: 'targetDeviceId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'Which of their devices' },
  { field: 'senderUserId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'omitted', note: 'Who sent it' },
  { field: 'senderDeviceId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'omitted', note: 'Which device sent it' },
  { field: 'messageType', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'Handshake or steady-state' },
  { field: 'timestamp', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'When it was sent' },
  { field: 'groupId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'Present on group messages' },
  { field: 'clientMessageId', identified: 'visible', sealed: 'visible', note: 'Retry idempotency key' },
  {
    field: 'ciphertext',
    identified: 'opaque',
    sealed: 'opaque',
    note: 'Bytes the relay never needs to read',
  },
];

/*
 * Two rows left this list and one arrived. "Who is receiving" restated the
 * targetUserId and targetDeviceId rows of the table directly above, and "Group
 * context" restated the groupId row.
 *
 * What stood in their place is the strongest published limit there is, and the
 * page did not carry it: docs/positioning.md §6 requires that one-sided
 * anonymity does not compose over a conversation. NDSS 2021 linked
 * sealed-sender users in as few as five messages using delivery receipts, and
 * follow-up work extended the technique to whole group conversations. This
 * page is the only surface anywhere that states sealed sender's limits —
 * docs/SECURITY.md in the SDK contains no occurrence of the word.
 */
const notHidden = [
  {
    title: 'Anonymity does not compose over a conversation',
    body: 'One-sided anonymity breaks when two parties exchange several messages. NDSS 2021 research linked sealed-sender users after as few as five messages, using delivery receipts. Later work extended the technique to whole group conversations. Whether your application supplies that signal is your design decision.',
  },
  {
    title: 'When, and roughly how much',
    body: 'Timestamps are plaintext. Message bodies are padded into 160-byte buckets, which coarsens length without concealing it. Attachments travel through a separate object store.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Network-level metadata',
    body: 'Addresses, connection timing, and traffic patterns belong to your transport and deployment. This SDK implements no decoy traffic and no timing jitter.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Sealed sender is best effort',
    body: 'If the recipient profile key or identity key is unavailable, the client falls back to identified delivery rather than failing the send.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security model"
  description="What a relay adapter receives field by field, what sealed sender does not hide, the threat model boundary, and the published limits."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Security model</p>
      {/* "written down" comments on the existence of the page to a reader who
          is on it. What the heading has to do is name the question the page
          answers, which is the question the lead under it answers verbatim. */}
      <h1>What your relay can see</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The relay never needs message plaintext or device private keys. It does need routing
        metadata.
      </p>
      <p class="maturity">{maturityLine}</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  {/*
    "Where the boundary sits" was a whole band of preamble: one h2 and one
    sentence asserting what the table below then proves with field names and
    two columns. Its second clause — "everything the relay holds is sealed, and
    everything sealed still carries an address" — was a parallel that added no
    fact the table does not carry. The diagram stays on this page and moves
    into the band that proves its point.

    The intro's own second clause went the same way: "it does not empty the
    envelope" rebutted a claim no reader arrived with.
  */}
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>The delivery envelope, field by field</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          Plaintext and long-term private keys exist only inside a device. This is what a relay
          adapter receives. Sealed sender omits two of these fields.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="diagram">
        <SignatureDiagram />
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Field</th>
              <th scope="col">Identified delivery</th>
              <th scope="col">Sealed sender</th>
              <th scope="col">What it is</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {envelopeFields.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>{row.field}</code></th>
                <td>{row.identified}</td>
                <td>{row.sealed}</td>
                <td>{row.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted measure">
        Some fields appear only in context: <code>recipientRegistrationId</code> accompanies the
        initial handshake envelope, and <code>contentHint</code>, <code>ephemeral</code>, and{' '}
        <code>urgent</code> are optional delivery hints. Your relay also assigns its own message id
        and server timestamp.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>What sealed sender does not hide</h2>
        {/* The intro hard-coded the row count and closed on "because a security
            page that only lists wins is marketing", which is the page
            congratulating itself in the middle of its own limits section.
            messaging.md §2 bans "anonymous" and "untraceable" applied to
            sealed sender, so the sentence that replaces it states the
            negation the rows then support. */}
        <p class="measure">
          Sealed sender omits the sender fields. It does not make a sender anonymous.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="rows">
        {notHidden.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    The in-scope and out-of-scope lists are gone, 117 words. They were a
    transcription of docs/SECURITY.md in the SDK, which this page links twice
    and about which this page says the documents win any disagreement. The copy
    had already drifted twice: the in-scope malformed-input row dropped Braid
    traces and Reed-Solomon chunks, and the out-of-scope first-contact row
    dropped the SDK's disjunction, making the site stricter than the SDK. A
    reviewer answering a questionnaire has to cite the repository document
    anyway.

    The relay formula stood here a second time; the hero carries it. Dropping
    it here is safe for the guard, which matches the period form the hero has.

    What stays is the trust-on-first-use callout, because it is the admission
    this page exists to make and the one thing here that is not the title of a
    linked section.
  */}
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Threat model boundary</h2>
        <p class="measure">
          An attacker in full control of your relay can serve stale or hostile responses and observe
          every routing field above. Once trust is pinned, an identity substitution that would reach
          plaintext fails closed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout measure">
        <p>
          First contact is trust on first use. A relay that is hostile from the first message can
          substitute an identity before trust is pinned. A safety-number comparison closes that gap.
          After trust is pinned, substituting either half of the identity tuple fails closed until a
          rotation is explicitly accepted.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href={docs.securityModel}>Threat model</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>What there is to check</h2>
        {/* "Everything above is a claim" undercut the four bands before it, and
            the tail was an aphorism — "because an undated number stops being
            evidence the moment it ages" — on a band whose headline figure was
            24 days old when it was written. The figures carry their own dates,
            so the intro only has to say that they do. */}
        <p class="measure">Each figure carries the date it was measured.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="figure-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>{checks.assertions}</dt>
          <dd>
            assertions across {checks.modules} modules, {checks.failed} failing and {checks.skipped}{' '}
            skipped, on {checks.measuredOn}. An export is only cut from a revision where they pass.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>{dependencies.direct}</dt>
          <dd>
            direct production dependencies: {dependencies.names.join(', ')}. They resolve to{' '}
            {dependencies.resolved} packages in total. The only edges inside the tree run between
            the @noble packages. Everything else is a development or optional peer dependency.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>{specifications.length}</dt>
          <dd>
            published specifications the implementation profile is pinned to, by revision, in the{' '}
            <a href={SDK_REPO}>SDK README</a>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>{reporting.acknowledgment}</dt>
          <dd>
            to acknowledge a vulnerability report sent to{' '}
            <a href={`mailto:${reporting.address}`}>{reporting.address}</a>, and{' '}
            {reporting.assessment} to an initial assessment. Do not open a public issue. The full
            policy is <a href={docs.reporting}>in the repository</a>.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      {/*
        An 85-word paragraph naming known-answer checks, conformance scenarios,
        property-based checks, integration flows, adapter suites and the
        executed quickstart stood here. It mirrored ASSURANCE.md's own coverage
        list, and the link to ASSURANCE.md is on the next line. Its opening
        clause gave the reason to cut it: "in the terms used elsewhere on this
        site" is a vocabulary reconciliation, which is a documentation job.
      */}
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href={docs.assurance}>Assurance</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    "Specifications, pinned by revision" stood here: a seven-row table whose
    own intro said "Mirrored from the SDK README, which is the maintained
    source." A mirror of a maintained source, on a page whose stated rule is
    that the documents win, is a maintenance liability with no reader benefit.
    The other mirror on this page is the assertion figure, and that is the one
    that went 24 days stale. The figure above now points at the README instead.

    All seven rows were verified current on the day they were cut, which is
    the moment the cut costs nothing. `specifications` stays in assurance.mjs:
    the figure reads its length, and the test suite asserts every row still
    carries a pinned revision.
  */}
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Published limits</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>Not audited by any independent firm</dt>
          <dd>
            Reviewed continuously by adversarial AI agents; not audited by any independent firm.
            Every change passes an adversarial AI review before it merges, and recurring
            whole-codebase AI audit passes run against the engineering repository. The{' '}
            <a href={docs.assurance}>assurance summary</a> states what that covers and what it does
            not. No independent firm has audited the SDK, and none is engaged.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Not a validated cryptographic module</dt>
          <dd>
            Pure-JavaScript cryptography is not FIPS 140-validated, and no part of this SDK is
            offered as a validated module. PQXDH uses FIPS 203 ML-KEM-1024. Braid mode uses a
            modified derivation, so no FIPS conformance claim holds there either. If your programme
            requires a validated module, this SDK does not meet it.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>JavaScript is not a constant-time environment</dt>
          <dd>
            Browser, Node, and React Native engines provide no machine-level constant-time
            guarantee. Selected paths use best-effort patterns. The SDK claims no timing
            equivalence against a co-resident adversary.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Memory cannot be reliably wiped</dt>
          <dd>
            Typed-array overwrites shorten the life of some buffers. Strings, engine temporaries,
            registers, and garbage-collected pages remain outside the SDK&rsquo;s control.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Post-quantum means confidentiality, not signatures</dt>
          <dd>
            Post-quantum key agreement is the default and fails closed, using ML-KEM-1024 for PQXDH
            and an ML-KEM Braid ratchet. One explicit opt-in,{' '}
            <code>protocol.postQuantum: 'compatible'</code>, permits a classical session only when a
            peer advertises no post-quantum material. Identity signatures remain classical Ed25519
            and X25519.
            The protection is against harvest-now-decrypt-later, not against a quantum adversary
            forging identities.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>There is no key escrow, so device loss is real</dt>
          <dd>
            Nothing recoverable is held on a server. Transferring state to new hardware requires
            the old device. A lost device means re-registering, and contacts see a safety-number
            change.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Not Signal Messenger, and not wire-compatible with it</dt>
          <dd>
            Messages, identities, and sessions do not cross between this SDK and Signal Messenger.
            Safety numbers shown here never match the ones a Signal Messenger client displays.{' '}
            <a href={`${DOCS}/docs/DEVIATIONS.md`}>DEVIATIONS</a> lists every difference and what it
            costs.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container stack">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>Read the primary sources</h2>
        {/* Was 34 words in one sentence, and it opened by announcing its own
            audience to that audience. Two of its three clauses said the same
            thing. The precedence rule is the part worth keeping, and it is
            what licenses every cut on this page. */}
        <p class="measure">
          These are the maintained documents. Where one disagrees with this page, the document is
          right.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="rows rows-single">
        <li><a href={`${DOCS}/docs/SECURITY.md`}>Threat model</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS}/docs/PROTOCOL_POLICY.md`}>Protocol policy and post-quantum modes</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS}/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS}/docs/E2EE.md`}>How the encryption works</a></li>
        <li><a href={docs.deviations}>DEVIATIONS</a></li>
        {/* Both this entry and the figures band's link to the same file used to
            carry the sentence "what runs, what is not published, and why". A
            link label is a promise about a destination, and the footer already
            sets one short name per destination; this list now uses those names
            and lets the documents describe themselves. */}
        <li>
          <a href={docs.assurance}>Assurance</a>
        </li>
        <li><a href={docs.thirdParty}>Third-party notices</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS}/SECURITY.md`}>Reporting a vulnerability</a></li>
        <li><a href={docs.license}>LICENSE</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    The page ended on two links of the same weight as the sentences around them,
    which left the reader who had just finished the threat model with nothing to
    do. The call to action leads because the person who reads this page to the
    end is usually reading it on someone else's behalf.

    That call used to be "get the security review pack", pointing at /evaluate.
    There is no /evaluate now: it asked the same questions this page answers and
    rendered the same `specifications` array from the same module, so a reader
    who followed the link arrived at a restatement of the page they had just
    finished. This page is the pack. What only /evaluate offered was the way out
    of documents entirely — a scoped review under an enterprise agreement — and
    that is the action here now.
  */}
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container measure stack">
      <h2>Take this to whoever signs it off</h2>
      {/* The paragraph that opened here restated the band directly above it —
          "this page is a summary, these are the documents it summarises" —
          nineteen lines later and with a semicolon. The second paragraph
          closed on "That is a conversation rather than a checkout, and it
          starts with a meeting", which is the button underneath it. */}
      <p>
        Enterprise and OEM agreements include a scoped security review, service levels, and
        negotiated portfolio or redistribution rights.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="oe-button" href="https://console.open-e2ee.dev/contact?plan=enterprise">
          Plan a security review
        </a>
        <a class="oe-button oe-button-secondary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
          Build your first encrypted message
        </a>
      </div>
      {/*
        /evaluate linked /licensing and /legal/terms inline, and folding it left
        the reviewer who has to answer "what may we ship this under" with two
        buttons that both assume the answer is already agreed. /licensing rather
        than /pricing because the question at this point is which license
        governs, not what a tier costs — and /licensing carries the /legal/terms
        link itself, so this restores the whole path rather than half of it.
      */}
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/licensing">Licensing</a>
      </p>
      <p class="muted">
        Email <a href="mailto:security@open-e2ee.dev">security@open-e2ee.dev</a> with a
        threat-model question, or an answer you need for a questionnaire. No license, meeting, or
        account first. If you are earlier than that, read{' '}
        <a href="/blog/e2ee-changes-your-application-architecture/"
          >E2EE changes your application architecture</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
